  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, under the Economic and Social Infrastructure Program (ESIP) is piloting the use of a climate risk and vulnerability assessment (CRVA) tool to respond to climate risks. The tool identifies and ranks climate and disaster risks and recommends adaptation measures to infrastructure design or construction to mitigate high priority risks. 
&#x200d;
ESIP piloted the CRVA tool on Stage 1 of its Arawa power station upgrade project. The tool identified numerous risks and mitigation opportunities. For example, additional hydrological modelling work will detail possible future river flood levels so that the site&#x2019;s drainage and solar panel height can be adjusted to accommodate future flood risk. The CRVA tool also identified overheating risks for key technical equipment that may result in reduced performance or infrastructure failure. The design team is now considering how to shade or mechanically cool the station&#x2019;s step-up transformers.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023, under the Economic and Social Infrastructure Program (ESIP), Australia supported the PNG Department of Information and Communications Technology (DICT) to develop its first ever Gender Equity and Social Inclusion (GESI) Policy. 
&#x200d;
The approved policy and implementation framework is being rolled out in 2024 through the leadership of DICT&#x2019;s GESI Manager. The GESI Manager supports an environment that enables women to actively contribute to DICT&#x2019;s mission to deliver effective, equitable and accessible digital services. 
&#x200d;
All DICT staff are trained in gender equity, disability and social inclusion and safeguards, as part of the GESI framework. ESIP supported the first training workshop in June 2024, which introduced key values, principles and approaches to mainstreaming the GESI Policy within DICT. 
&#x200d;
ESIP is providing ongoing mentoring and technical support to the Manager to assist her to continue championing GESI at DICT.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kimbe Market Redevelopment was completed in 2023. The market has more than 800 traders serving 2000 customers per day, making it a critical economic hub for the Kimbe region.
&#x200d;
The project was aligned to G20 Quality Infrastructure principles and Australian Government policies, providing transparency and value for money. 
&#x200d;
The construction fully met Australian standards, and PNG and Australian construction codes. Climate change and disaster risk considerations were actively factored into design to ensure the market could tolerate heavy rainfall events and included passive building design principles such as a white reflective roof to reduce heat load. ESIP monitored the contractor&#x2019;s health, safety and environment systems, keeping the construction site safe with no lost time due to injury. 
&#x200d;
The project&#x2019;s Gender Equity, Disability and Social Inclusion and Safeguards Action Plan facilitated employment and skills training opportunities for women and people with disability during construction.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The PNG-registered Tenkile Conservation Alliance received a grant for the Kamapim Pawa project through Australia&#x2019;s Pawarim Komuniti program. Over 4 years, the project distributed 8,005 solar household systems and 350 solar-powered streetlights to 146 villages in the Lumi, Aitape/Lumi District of Sandaun Province. The project provided basic lighting and energy services to 24,095 people (13,065 men, 11,030 women), including 109 people with disability. 
&#x200d;
Previously, most of these households had no access to electricity apart from some small solar and battery-powered torches. After the project, nearly all beneficiary households reported high levels of satisfaction with the new solar systems. Benefits reported by households included improved ability to charge and use mobile phones; reduced battery, phone charging and kerosene costs; and the ability to engage in new productive activities. Solar streetlights illuminated community areas, resulting in 80% of the population, including women and girls, feeling safer at night.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Working with the ADB, Australia helped the PNG Government implement a range of state-owned enterprise (SOE) reform actions aimed at improving governance and performance of PNG&#x2019;s SOEs. Australian technical assistance and support enabled the PNG Government to implement actions and complete a three-year ADB SOE reform program tied to a USD500 million loan. As part of this package of support, Australia also provided assistance to a number of SOEs, including PNG Power, for improved financial management, audits, oversight of reforms including corporate planning and management, and engagement with PNG Treasury.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
